WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:07.850
- Hello, everybody.

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:09.590
I'm Maurice Samuels,

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:11.430
the Director of the Yale Program

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:13.370
for the Study of Antisemitism.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:15.930
And it's my great pleasure
to welcome you today

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:17.480
to the next installment

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.900
in our Benjamin and Barbara
Zucker Lecture Series,

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.930
which is being co-sponsored today

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:25.970
by the University of California, Berkeley.

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:28.590
Our speaker is Ethan Katz.

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:32.620
He received his BA from
Amherst and his PhD in history

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:34.993
from the University of Wisconsin.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:37.350
Before moving to Berkeley,

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:39.990
he taught at the University of Cincinnati

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:42.200
and he is now Associate Professor

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:45.390
of History and Jewish Studies at Berkeley,

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:49.210
where he co-leads an anti-Semitism
education initiative,

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:50.743
which he recently founded.

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:54.700
A specialist of France
and the Francophone world,

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.690
Katz's research interests
include Jewish-Muslim relations,

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:02.380
Jews in colonial societies,
Holocaust studies

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:05.330
and the interplay between
the religious and secular

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:07.310
in modern Jewish life.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:08.417
His first book,

00:01:08.417 --> 00:01:11.477
"The Burdens of Brotherhood:
Jews and Muslims

00:01:11.477 --> 00:01:13.260
"from North Africa to France"

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:17.760
published by Harvard in 2015,
received a number of prizes,

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.180
including a National Jewish Book Award,

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.150
and the J. Russell Major Prize

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.960
of the American Historical Association.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.260
Katz has also co-edited three books.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:31.520
These include "Colonialism and the Jews",

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.470
honored last year as a finalist

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:35.380
for a National Jewish Book Award.

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:37.457
And most recently "A Special Issue

00:01:37.457 --> 00:01:40.667
"of the Journal Jewish
history on Jews and Muslims

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:45.373
"in France Before and After
Charlie Hebdo and Hyper Cacher".

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.500
he's now at work on an
exciting new book project

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:53.920
on the Jewish underground in
Algiers, during World War II,

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:56.637
currently entitled "Freeing the Empire:

00:01:56.637 --> 00:01:59.097
"The Jewish Uprising
Of Jews and Antisemites

00:01:59.097 --> 00:02:01.350
That Helped Win the War II."

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:05.620
And his lecture today draws
from research for this project.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:10.170
So I wanna urge all of
you to ask questions,

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:12.010
to write your questions at any point.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:15.500
You can start writing as
soon as he starts talking,

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.760
but submit those questions,
